../docs/en/device_dev_guide/driver/driver-troubleshooting-guide.md:151:### 1. Prefer Using work_queue for Deferred or Background Tasks
../docs/en/device_dev_guide/driver/driver-troubleshooting-guide.md:157:- **Solution:** You should prioritize using the system's `work_queue` mechanism. A `work_queue` uses shared kernel worker threads to execute the tasks (`work`) submitted to it, thereby reusing thread resources and significantly saving memory.
../docs/en/device_dev_guide/driver/driver-troubleshooting-guide.md:159:- **Related Documentation:** [Work Queue Development Guide](../kernel/IPC/work_queue.md)
../docs/en/device_dev_guide/driver/driver-troubleshooting-guide.md:161:### 2. Avoid Executing Long-Running Tasks in a work_queue
../docs/en/device_dev_guide/driver/driver-troubleshooting-guide.md:163:- **Symptom:** Other services in the system that depend on the `work_queue` (e.g., networking, USB) experience delays or become unresponsive.
../docs/en/device_dev_guide/driver/driver-troubleshooting-guide.md:165:- **Cause:** The `work_queue`'s worker threads are a shared system resource. All `work` items submitted to the same queue are executed sequentially. If your `work` item performs a long-running blocking operation, it will monopolize the worker thread, preventing other `work` items in the queue from being processed in a timely manner.
../docs/en/device_dev_guide/driver/driver-troubleshooting-guide.md:170:    - **Reschedule the work:** At the end of one sub-task, use `work_queue()` or `work_schedule()` to reschedule the next sub-task, thereby yielding the CPU to other `work` items.
